INT. BLACKWOOD LODGE - GREAT ROOM - NIGHT

A hunting lodge in deep snow. Antlers on every wall. A fire roars in a stone hearth. Outside, a blizzard howls.

PROFESSOR QUENTIN LAIRD, 60s, in a cardigan, stands before the fire. ODETTE, 30s, a journalist, sits cross-legged on a rug with a notebook. BASIL, 40s, the caretaker, nurses a whiskey at the bar. NGOZI, 30s, a doctor, stares at her phone, which shows no bars. HARLAN, 50s, in a camouflage vest, paces behind the sofa.

QUENTIN
(to everyone)
I appreciate your patience. As you can see, we are snowed in.

ODETTE
For how long?

BASIL
Two days. Maybe three.

NGOZI
(to herself)
No signal. Of course.

HARLAN
Who called us here?

QUENTIN
I did.

HARLAN
You said it was a reunion.

QUENTIN
It was.

HARLAN
Then why are we missing someone?

Nobody answers. The fire cracks.

ODETTE
(to Quentin)
You said the sixth guest would come at nine.

QUENTIN
He will.

BASIL
It's past eleven.

QUENTIN
He is very punctual, when he wants to be.

Ngozi pockets her phone. Harlan's pacing stops.

NGOZI
Quentin, who is he?

QUENTIN
Someone we all knew, a long time ago.

HARLAN
Name.

QUENTIN
Teddy Marsh.

Basil drops his glass. It shatters. Odette stops writing.

BASIL
(low)
Teddy died. Twenty years ago.

QUENTIN
Did he?

The front door rattles. Everyone turns. The wind howls, and the rattling stops. Quentin smiles faintly into the fire.

ODETTE
(to herself)
Stay calm. Stay calm.

NGOZI
Basil. Check the door.

BASIL
Why me?

NGOZI
Because you're the caretaker.

BASIL
It's not in my contract.

Harlan crosses the room and lays his hand on the latch. The rattling begins again, softer, and he draws back.

HARLAN
(to the room)
Does anyone else hear that?

Odette closes her notebook. Quentin sits, finally, in the leather chair, and folds his hands.

QUENTIN
That would be him.
